    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "More than seven million people die from coronary artery disease each year, making it the leading cause of death worldwide. Investigating the developmental origins of coronary arteries can lead to a better understanding of and improved therapeutics for coronary artery disease. Many aspects of coronary artery angiogenesis, however, remain unclear. Pericytes, mural cells closely associated with small blood vessels, have recently been identified as progenitors for coronary artery smooth muscle cells (caSMCs) (Volz et al., 2015). This pathway is in part controlled by Platelet Derived Growth Factor Receptor β (PDGFRβ), an important receptor for smooth muscle development that diminishes caSMC maturation upon deletion (Mellgren et al., 2008). Similarly, studies of the Notch3 receptor have shown that notch3 is involved in pericyte development and maintenance and promotes vascular smooth muscle cell proliferation (Greenwald, 1998). Our study analyzes the effects of the Notch3 receptor on the development of coronary artery smooth muscle in a murine model. Coronary artery smooth muscle develops when blood flow induces vessels within the vascular plexus, an immature vessel bed permeating the entire ventricular heart muscle, to remodel into mature coronary arteries with smooth muscle cell coating in regions called remodeling zones (Red-Horse et al., 2010; Kattan et al., 2004). Here, we show that the deletion of Notch3 disrupts caSMC differentiation at the coronary artery remodeling zone. The defect in smooth muscle, however, is likely not due to differential proliferation of pericytes between wild type and Notch3-null mice. We additionally characterize pericyte biology at sites of arterial differentiation and show that the remodeling zone contains significantly more pericytes than the vascular plexus, but there is no difference in proliferation at the former site. These data collectively indicate that pericytes give rise to coronary artery smooth muscle through an integral Notch signaling mechanism and that smooth muscle differentiation involves pericytes migrating into the remodeling zone rather than increased proliferation at the remodeling zone. This reveals a new potential pathway that can be therapeutically targeted during coronary artery disease.",
